## Title (working)

**Necessity, Not Convenience: Subspace-Restricted Zeroth-Order Fine-Tuning of Real Weights on Mobile NPUs**

*(Alternative: "Making Zeroth-Order Fine-Tuning of Real LLM Weights Possible on Mobile NPUs: A Co-Design Study")*

---

## Abstract

Fine-tuning LLMs on-device is attractive for personalization and privacy, but mobile NPUs
such as Qualcomm Hexagon are inference-only accelerators with no documented backward-pass
kernels — zeroth-order (ZO) optimization is therefore not a memory-saving choice on this
hardware class, it is the only viable one. A separate line of work restricts ZO's
perturbation to a learned low-rank subspace of a model's *real* weight matrices — bypassing
the need for a LoRA adapter entirely — but has only ever been validated in GPU simulation.
We deploy this method on real Hexagon NPU hardware for Qwen3-0.6B and report, to our
knowledge, the first on-device *accuracy* results for this method family; the closest prior
real-hardware ZO work (MobiZO) restricts via a persistent LoRA adapter and reports only
runtime/memory from its own on-device run, never accuracy. We show three things that are
only visible once the method is actually deployed, not simulated. First, naively extending
the batched-dispatch fusion that gives this method its speed to the *full* weight matrix —
rather than the small subspace coefficient — pushes 3.2GB through a single kernel dispatch
with no operand-size guard and crashes the device outright, requiring a firmware-level
recovery; subspace restriction is therefore a safety *requirement*, not merely an efficiency
choice. Second, subspace rank controls a genuine crossover between CPU and NPU as the faster
backend (9.1$\times$/10.5$\times$ NPU speedup at r=256 refresh/steady-state cost; NPU is
*slower* than CPU at r=8), driven by how well a given rank's per-step work amortizes fixed
NPU dispatch overhead. Third, moving the subspace-refresh SVD itself on-device (block power
iteration instead of host-side sequential deflation) turns a previously infeasible
operation at large rank into a practical one (50$\times$ faster at r=64; r=256 goes from
never finishing on host to 33s on-device). On-device, our subspace-restricted method matches
its own full-model, no-subspace NPU baseline (92.0% vs. 92.7%, SST-2, N=500 examples) and,
at matched sample count with the closest external comparison, our GPU-validated
configuration reaches accuracy statistically indistinguishable from MobiZO's own reported
number for this exact model and task (87.3% vs. 87.8%, N=1000) — while, unlike MobiZO,
reporting that number from work grounded in real weight updates rather than an adapter.

