## 1. Introduction

Fine-tuning large language models directly on a user's device is attractive for
personalization and privacy, avoiding any round trip of user data to a server. The obstacle
is not just memory — it is that the accelerators best suited to run an LLM on a phone at
all, mobile NPUs such as Qualcomm's Hexagon, are built and documented purely for inference:
their published programmer's references (HVX/HMX PRM) and architecture disclosures describe
no backward-pass or autodiff primitives. On a server GPU, zeroth-order (ZO) optimization
[MeZO; FZOO] is chosen *among alternatives* to save memory relative to backpropagation. On
an inference-only NPU, it is not a choice at all — it is the only path by which on-device
fine-tuning can happen.

A parallel line of work reduces ZO's core weakness — gradient-estimator variance that scales
with the number of parameters perturbed — by restricting the perturbation to a learned or
random low-rank subspace [P-GAP; SubZero; LOZO], applied directly to a model's real weight
matrices rather than an auxiliary adapter. This line of work is validated only in GPU
simulation. The one paper reporting real mobile-NPU measurements for ZO fine-tuning at all,
MobiZO, restricts via a persistent LoRA adapter and explicitly scopes its own on-device
numbers as runtime/correctness sanity checks — it never reports on-device task accuracy, for
any model.

We build a subspace-restricted ZO fine-tuning system for real Hexagon NPU hardware and
report the first on-device accuracy results for this method family. Doing so required
combining three pieces of prior work in a way none of them do individually, plus solving
three NPU-specific systems problems that only appear once the method is actually deployed:

**Algorithmic combination (Section 3).** P-GAP's subspace-identification mechanism (probe
+ periodic SVD of the real weight's approximate gradient) is paired with FZOO's
one-sided, Rademacher-perturbed estimator instead of P-GAP's own continuous, Gaussian-based
directional alignment — a substitution made specifically because alignment has no bit-valued
form and cannot be dispatched as a sign-flip on NPU hardware. A calibrated, precision-triggered
sample-count controller (adapted from the classical norm-test literature, and never before
applied to this specific one-sided estimator) is then coupled to the *same* frozen reference
statistic that also decides when the subspace itself needs an early refresh — one calibration
pass, zero extra hyperparameters, driving two independent decisions.

**Systems contributions (Section 4).** (i) We show, empirically, that this is not an optional
optimization: naively fusing the dispatch of a full-rank real-weight update crashes real
hardware. (ii) We move the subspace-refresh SVD itself on-device via batched block power
iteration, turning an operation that never completes at practical rank on host into one that
does. (iii) We show subspace rank determines which backend — CPU or NPU — is even faster,
evidence the algorithm's operating point and the hardware's cost structure are coupled, not
independent.

Together, these are, to our knowledge: the first real on-device accuracy measurement for
non-adapter, subspace-restricted ZO fine-tuning; the first documented real-hardware failure
mode showing subspace restriction is a necessity rather than a convenience for this class of
deployment; and the first characterization of a rank-driven CPU/NPU crossover for this
method family.

