## 2. Related Work

**Zeroth-order fine-tuning.** MeZO [1] perturbs all parameters with a two-point estimator at
inference-level memory cost. FZOO [2] replaces Gaussian perturbations with a batched,
one-sided Rademacher estimator normalized by observed loss variance, reducing forward-pass
count 3-18$\times$ over MeZO. Neither restricts the perturbed dimensionality.

**Subspace-restricted ZO.** SubZero [3] and LOZO [4] restrict perturbations to a random
low-rank subspace, provably reducing estimator variance with rank. P-GAP [5] instead learns
the subspace from probed gradient information via periodic SVD, and further aligns the
perturbation direction to it — reporting strong gains, but only on GPU, and only for a
continuous, Gaussian-based alignment mechanism incompatible with bit-level (Rademacher)
perturbation. Dominant-Layer-ZO [6] restricts to a single activation-outlier layer instead of
a subspace; the mechanism is model-specific and, in our own replication on the model family
used here, the activation-outlier heuristic did not identify a genuine outlier layer at all.

**Real-hardware ZO deployment.** MobiZO [7] is, to our knowledge, the only prior work
reporting ZO fine-tuning measurements on real mobile hardware (a Jetson Orin Nano and a
Qualcomm-NPU phone), restricting via a LoRA-FA adapter. Its real-device experiments (Android
NPU) report only a per-step loss-matching sanity check and runtime/memory — never on-device
accuracy, for any model, including the sub-1B models it separately evaluates
(Llama3.2-1B/Qwen3-0.6B/SmolLM-340M) in a GPU-only appendix. [8] separately argues ZO
fine-tuning has the computational signature of an inference workload and demonstrates
speedups by rescheduling it onto a GPU serving stack — a scheduling argument, not a
hardware-restriction one, and not evaluated on NPU hardware.

**This work** combines P-GAP's gradient-informed subspace mechanism with FZOO's
hardware-compatible Rademacher estimator, applied to real weight matrices, and is, to our
knowledge, the first to report real on-device accuracy for any subspace-restricted,
non-adapter ZO method. Concurrent work [11] applies the same Rademacher-perturbed ZO gradient
primitive to a different problem — one-shot sensitivity estimation for post-training
quantization of vision-language models — underscoring that ZO's forward-pass-only structure
is increasingly recognized as a lever for hardware-constrained problems beyond fine-tuning;
its use of the estimator is diagnostic and GPU-only, with no deployment component and no
technical overlap with this paper's use of ZO as the training algorithm itself.

