## 3. Method

**Base estimator.** For a real weight matrix $W \in \mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$ restricted to a
rank-$r$ subspace with orthonormal bases $U_r, V_r$ (refreshed every $k$ steps from $h$ probe
perturbations via SVD of the resulting approximate gradient), each step draws $N$ Rademacher
directions $Z_i \in \{-1,+1\}^{r\times r}$ and, via a forward hook on the target module,
computes

$$v = x V_r,\quad \text{coeff}_i = Z_i v,\quad \hat{y}_i = y + \epsilon\,(\text{coeff}_i U_r^\top)$$

with the FZOO step normalized by the in-batch loss standard deviation $\sigma_t$ (unchanged
from [2]). $W$ itself is never touched during the $N$ perturbed forward passes — only during
the actual update step, and only during subspace refresh. The accumulated update is scaled by

$$\mu = \sqrt{mn}/r$$

to compensate the Frobenius-norm shrinkage of restricting randomness to an $r\times r$
coefficient rather than the full $m\times n$ space — an $r\times r$ coefficient accumulates
only $r^2$ independent Rademacher signs per step, versus $mn$ for an unrestricted update at
the same $N$; without this term the same learning rate is silently up to $20\times$ too small
for the restricted update.

**Why Rademacher, not P-GAP's own alignment estimator.** P-GAP's directional alignment
projection is a continuous, Gaussian-based correction with no bit-valued output — it cannot
be made to produce a $\pm1$ result without reverting to floating-point sampling and real
multiplies, discarding the 1-bit-RNG, sign-flip, no-multiply arithmetic that makes Rademacher
perturbation dispatchable on NPU hardware at all. We inherit only P-GAP's
subspace-identification mechanism (probe + SVD) and pair it with FZOO's estimator — this
substitution is what makes a gradient-informed subspace usable on this hardware class in the
first place, not a straightforward reimplementation of either paper alone.

**Adaptive control.** A calibration pass over the first $c$ steps at a fixed sample count
freezes a reference statistic $\sigma_{\text{ref}}$ (median observed loss standard deviation)
and a floor $\sigma_{\text{floor}} = f\cdot\sigma_{\text{ref}}$. Thereafter, $N$ escalates from
$N_{\min}$ toward $N_{\max}$ while the standard error of the current estimate,
$\mathrm{SE}\approx\hat\sigma/\sqrt{2(N-1)}$, exceeds $\tau\cdot\sigma_{\text{ref}}$ (unless
$\hat\sigma$ has already dropped below $0.5\,\sigma_{\text{ref}}$). If $N_{\max}$ is reached
without meeting this precision test, a subspace refresh is forced at the start of the next
step rather than mid-step, keeping every step's worst-case cost bounded. This single frozen
statistic therefore drives two decisions — how many directions to sample, and when the
subspace itself needs refreshing — from one calibration pass, with zero additional
hyperparameters. This coupling of an adaptive-sampling controller to the subspace-refresh
schedule, specifically for FZOO's one-sided estimator, does not appear in prior work: the
closest precedent (AdaZeta [9]) uses a fixed sublinear query schedule for a two-point
(MeZO-style) estimator and does not couple to a subspace-refresh decision at all.

