## 4. Why This Belongs on an NPU, Not Just Runs on One

**(P1) Necessity, architecturally.** Hexagon's own programmer's references document no
backward/autodiff primitives — ZO is not selected among alternatives on this hardware, it is
the only fine-tuning path available at all.

**(P2) Necessity, empirically.** A naive dispatch-fused implementation of full-rank
real-weight ZO training — stacking $N{+}1$ full perturbed copies of every target weight
tensor through a single kernel dispatch, exactly mirroring how our method instead fuses the
tiny *subspace coefficient* — pushed **3.2GB** through a DSP kernel path with no
operand-size guard, previously exercised only at LoRA-adapter (KB) scale, and **crashed the
device into firmware recovery mode**, requiring a manual reboot. Subspace restriction keeps
the fused coefficient tiny ($r{\times}r{\times}n_{\text{slots}}$, a few KB regardless of $r$)
and is what makes the fused path safe, not merely fast — the fix (a hard size guard on the
underlying batched-matmul dispatch primitive) is now permanent, but the failure mode itself
is the argument: subspace restriction was never optional for this deployment target.

**(P3) A genuine rank/hardware crossover.** At $r{=}256$: refresh cost drops from 452.4s
(host, sequential single-vector deflation SVD) to 49.5s (**9.1$\times$**) and steady-state
per-step cost drops from 28.9s to 2.75s (**10.5$\times$**), NPU vs. CPU. At $r{=}8$: NPU is
*slower* than CPU (~1.35s/step vs. ~0.92s/step) — fixed per-dispatch overhead dominates a
workload too small to amortize it. The same rank knob that trades accuracy for capacity also
determines which backend is faster at all. `[PENDING: clean fixed-N=8 rank sweep at
r∈{8,32,64,128,256}, both backends, currently running — will replace the two-point
characterization above with a full curve and locate the exact crossover rank]`.

**(P4) On-device SVD as a systems contribution, not just a speedup.** The host-side subspace
refresh (sequential, single-vector power iteration with deflation) does not merely run
slower at large rank — it does not complete in practical time at all: $r{=}256$ never
finished after several minutes and had to be killed. Replacing it with a batched block power
iteration — computing all $r$ directions for every target tensor simultaneously via ordinary
full-size matmuls the model's own forward pass already uses safely, rather than one
`mul_mat_id`-dispatched tiny operation at a time — brings $r{=}64$'s refresh cost from 270s to
5.4s (**50$\times$**) and turns $r{=}256$ from infeasible into a practical 33s. This is an
algorithm change enabled by the hardware's own batched-matmul throughput, not a
re-implementation of the same algorithm running faster.

**(P5) Kernel fusion one level deeper than prior real-hardware work.** MobiZO's own
dispatch-fusion mechanism (MP-LoRA) batches multiple perturbed forward passes by stacking
full per-direction LoRA-$B$ matrices. Because our perturbation is already restricted to an
$r\times r$ coefficient, our fusion stacks *that* — a granularity one level smaller than
MobiZO's adapter-matrix-level batching, for the same reason (P2) makes it necessary: fusing
anything larger than a subspace coefficient at real-weight scale is what crashes the device.

