## 7. Conclusion

Subspace-restricted zeroth-order fine-tuning of a model's real weights, deployed on real
Hexagon NPU hardware, is the first work in this method family to report on-device accuracy
at all — matching its own full-model on-device baseline and reaching accuracy
statistically indistinguishable, at matched sample count, from the closest prior real-NPU ZO
work's own reported number, while doing so through real weight updates rather than an
adapter. Getting there required treating subspace restriction as a hardware safety
requirement rather than an optional speedup: the naive alternative crashes the device.
Beyond accuracy, we show subspace rank determines which backend is even faster at all, and
that moving the subspace-identification SVD itself on-device turns a previously infeasible
operation into a practical one — evidence that this method was shaped by, not merely
deployed onto, this class of accelerator.

---

## 8. Future Work

**Quantization-compatible training.** The current design requires the subspace *target*
tensor to be stored at full precision during training (to accumulate small per-step
updates without rounding them away), while every other tensor stays quantized. A natural
extension keeps the target tensor itself quantized permanently by never merging the learned
correction back into it — instead applying the same activation-projection mechanism already
used during training ($v = xV_r$, $\text{coeff}\, U_r^\top$) at inference time as a small,
persistent additive correction. This would let the entire backbone, including the trained
tensors, stay in the deployed quantization format throughout, at the cost of moving the
method's deployment shape closer to a persistent adapter than a merged real-weight update —
a trade-off worth exploring once the current accuracy/systems results are locked down.

